ユーラシア大陸の深部に位置するonva（ア゛ンヴァ）。1928年、不合理な言語と複雑な社会システムを廃し、18文字のbyam（ビャ文字）を核とした「最適化」を掲げて建国されました。=Located deep in Eurasia, onva was founded in 1928 around optimisation: replacing irrational language and complex social systems with the 18-character byam script.
合理主義による言語の再構築=Rebuilding language through rationalism
onvaは、既存の言語の冗長性を排除するために1928年に誕生しました。住民は主に白人系で構成され、論理的な思考と機能美を何よりも尊重します。 彼らが使用する18文字のbyamは、情報の伝達コストを最小化するために設計された究極の最適化ツールです。=Founded in 1928 to eliminate linguistic redundancy, onva values logical thought and functional beauty. Its population is predominantly of European ancestry. Its 18-character byam script is designed to minimise the cost of communication.
onva出身のデジタルアート作家。記号を用いた芸術表現や、独自の感性に基づく世界構築を行っています。=A digital artist from onva, creating symbolic art and worlds shaped by a distinctive imagination.
@intro=onva is a kingdom founded in East Calendar 1928. Its society and culture pursue rational design and optimization.

@conversation-grammar=<h2>Conversation grammar · Revision 2</h2><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Writing and boundaries</h3><p>Separate words with spaces and use hyphens inside compounds and affixed words. Keep punctuation. Proper names may retain their original spelling. Use subject–verb–object order; omit a subject only in an unambiguous reply.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye m-i m.</span><code translate="no">sye m-i m.</code><span>I drink water.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Nouns, adjectives and “be”</h3><p>Keep t-t before a noun or adjective predicate. Adjectives and quantities precede nouns. f-sye / f-yu mean this / that; after a noun they mean this / that noun. Use quantities or won-r (people) when plurality matters. Do not apply verbal -p to nouns.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye t-t !anne-won.</span><code translate="no">sye t-t !anne-won.</code><span>I am a student.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">f-bya f-sye t-t b.</span><code translate="no">f-bya f-sye t-t b.</code><span>This book is good.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Questions and commands</h3><p>For yes/no questions, keep SVO and append wo-wo. Replace an unknown noun with wo-f (what) or wo-won (who). Use z-e / te-z wo-z for where and eti-z wo-eti for when. Put wo-n!ag (why) or wo-ritt (how) last. Content questions need no wo-wo. Reserve initial wo for commands; h-b wo makes a polite request. Use wo n-verb for prohibitions.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">yu i wo-wo?</span><code translate="no">yu i wo-wo?</code><span>Do you eat?</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">wo-won i i-z-m?</span><code translate="no">wo-won i i-z-m?</code><span>Who eats potato salad?</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">h-b wo r-r bya f-yu.</span><code translate="no">h-b wo r-r bya f-yu.</code><span>Please say that again.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Negation, tense and aspect</h3><p>Negate the entire verb with n-. Affix order: n- + stem + passive -ep + aspect (-u or -!abh) + tense (-p or -e). Unmarked verbs use present, habitual or contextual time. -p is past, -e future, -u progressive, -!abh perfect. Choose at most one aspect and one tense. Passive -ep never means ability. Put !abh before the verb for just now. Use n-!abh plus a negated verb for not yet.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye n-i-p i-z-m.</span><code translate="no">sye n-i-p i-z-m.</code><span>I did not eat potato salad.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye i-u i-z-m.</span><code translate="no">sye i-u i-z-m.</code><span>I am eating potato salad.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">f-bya ne-ep-p.</span><code translate="no">f-bya ne-ep-p.</code><span>The book was seen.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Want, ability and need</h3><p>rep means want/request, h-te ability, and h-n need. Nominalize an action by attaching ti- to its verb stem, including its following object and prepositional phrases. Its understood subject is the main subject. Use a content clause for a different subject. n-h-te means cannot; h-te ti-n-verb means can refrain from doing.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye rep m.</span><code translate="no">sye rep m.</code><span>I want water.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye rep ti-te te-z z-h-won.</span><code translate="no">sye rep ti-te te-z z-h-won.</code><span>I want to go home.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye n-h-te ti-bya !anbyam.</span><code translate="no">sye n-h-te ti-bya !anbyam.</code><span>I cannot speak Anbyam.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Possession, place, recipient and time</h3><p>Use noun yu-h owner for possession. Put prepositional phrases after the object: z-e location, te-z destination, ti-z source, yu-e recipient, eti-z time, in that order when more than one occurs.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye h f-bya yu-h yu.</span><code translate="no">sye h f-bya yu-h yu.</code><span>I have your book.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye h-yu f-yu yu-e yu.</span><code translate="no">sye h-yu f-yu yu-e yu.</code><span>I give that to you.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye te-e te-z z-!anne eti-z eti-e-m.</span><code translate="no">sye te-e te-z z-!anne eti-z eti-e-m.</code><span>I will go to school tomorrow.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Connecting clauses and reporting thoughts</h3><p>A, t-tr B means A, therefore B. !abu A, t-tr B means if A, then B. yu-r means and; n-p means but. Enclose a content clause with bya-t and bya-n. It also uses SVO; close nested clauses from the inside out.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye !ann bya-t won ti-e bya-n.</span><code translate="no">sye !ann bya-t won ti-e bya-n.</code><span>I think they will come.</span></div><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">!abu yu ti, t-tr sye h-yu f-bya yu-e yu.</span><code translate="no">!abu yu ti, t-tr sye h-yu f-bya yu-e yu.</code><span>If you come, I will give you a book.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Relative clauses such as “the book I bought”</h3><p>After a noun, write bya-f SVO bya-n. Put wo-f exactly once in the role filled by that noun. Within this clause, wo-f refers to the preceding noun and is not a question. Keeping the slot explicit distinguishes subjects, objects and locations.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">f-yu t-t f-bya bya-f sye ne-h-p wo-f bya-n.</span><code translate="no">f-yu t-t f-bya bya-f sye ne-h-p wo-f bya-n.</code><span>That is the book I bought.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Numbers and units</h3><p>Join a multiplier and place into one word: p-uwo-t is 2×10. Add space-separated place groups: wo-t h-t is 18; p-uwo-t is 20. Use f-t consistently for six. Quantities precede nouns.</p><div class="grammar-example"><span class="onva-r" translate="no">sye h wo-t h-t byam.</span><code translate="no">sye h wo-t h-t byam.</code><span>I have eighteen letters.</span></div></article><article class="grammar-card"><h3>Changes from the earlier guide</h3><p>The revision replaces question inversion with wo-wo and explicit question words. wo marks commands (its adjective sense wide remains). Replace sye-!abh i with sye !abh i-p, and yuiwo with yu i wo-f? Separate concatenated sentences and restore t-t before noun/adjective predicates. ti-n-wor means not sleeping; n-ti-wor means not the act of sleeping. Negation scope follows prefix order.</p></article>
